NAME
    label - attach symbolic names to addresses

SYNOPSIS
    label add <addr> <name> [<comment...>]
    label list [<filter>]
    label at <addr>
    label remove <name|addr>
    label clear
    label load <path>
    label save <path>

DESCRIPTION
    Associates symbolic names (and optional comments) with hex addresses
    in the attached session. Labelled addresses display as
    '$00A3B2 <NmiHandler>' across disasm(1), bt(1), bp(1), counters(1),
    profile(1), and vectors(1), and are usable as symbols in eval(1) and
    breakpoint expressions (for example, 'bp add NmiHandler' or
    'eval [PlayerX]').

    The mapping is one-to-one: renaming an address replaces its previous
    name, and assigning an existing name to a new address moves the name.

OPTIONS
    add <addr> <name> [<comment...>]
        Bind <name> (with optional comment text) to hex address <addr>.

    list [<filter>]
        List defined labels in address order, optionally filtered by
        case-insensitive substring <filter>.

    at <addr>
        Print the exact label at <addr>, or the nearest label below <addr>
        with byte offset (for example, 'MainLoop+12').

    remove <name|addr>
        Remove a label by name or hex address.

    clear
        Remove all labels from the session.

    load <path>
        Merge labels from <path> (resolved inside the shell sandbox).

    save <path>
        Write all current labels to <path> (resolved inside the shell
        sandbox).

FILE FORMAT
    Plain text, one entry per line: '<hex address> <name> [comment...]'.
    Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored. Optional '$' or
    '0x' prefixes on addresses are accepted. Paths resolve inside the
    shell sandbox (see hier(7)).

EXAMPLES
    label add 00A3B2 NmiHandler main vblank entry
    label add 7E13C6 CoinCount
    label at 00A3C0
    label list Nmi
    label save /Logs/smw.labels
    label load /Logs/smw.labels

EXIT STATUS
    0   Operation completed (or usage message printed).
    1   No target attached, labels unsupported by core, path outside
        sandbox, invalid hex address, or I/O/session fault.

SEE ALSO
    disasm(1), bt(1), bp(1), eval(1), callers(1), vectors(1), hier(7)
